Material degradation acceptance acknowledges that patina, weathering, and aging are natural and beautiful aspects of product use rather than failures requiring replacement. By designing materials and finishes that develop character and visual interest through aging—copper patina, weathered wood grain, worn leather texture—products become more meaningful and valued over time. This strategy directly contradicts fast fashion and disposable product aesthetics that require pristine, unchanging appearance. Products designed for graceful aging demonstrate durability while building emotional attachment through visible evidence of longevity and shared history.